BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Fellowship 2027-2028 for Artists\, Scholars and T
 hinkers (Cassis\, France)
DESCRIPTION:The Camargo Foundation invites artists\, scholars and thinkers 
 to its flagship Core Fellowship: a ten-week self-directed residency on its
  clifftop campus overlooking the Mediterranean in Cassis\, France\, dedica
 ted to research\, experimentation and interdisciplinary exchange. Curators
  apply under the Thinkers category\, which covers accomplished professiona
 ls and practitioners in cultural and creative fields who are professionall
 y engaged in critical thought\, including curators\, critics and journalis
 ts\; projects should engage theoretical\, artistic or societal questions. 
 WHAT YOU GET: a stipend of EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 total)\, basic tra
 nsportation funding\, and a furnished apartment with an equipped kitchen o
 n campus. Fourteen fellowships are awarded per year: seven for artists and
  seven for scholars and thinkers. RESIDENCY PERIODS: Fall 2027 (8 Septembe
 r to 17 November 2027) or Spring 2028 (23 February to 4 May 2028). ELIGIBI
 LITY: artists in visual arts\, performance\, writing\, film\, music or mul
 tidisciplinary work with at least 5 years of professional practice\; schol
 ars holding or pursuing a PhD focused on French and Francophone cultures o
 r cross-cultural Mediterranean studies\; and thinkers as described above. 
 APPLICATION: via Submittable with a 100-word proposal summary\, a 1\,200-w
 ord proposal narrative\, a 300-word rationale for Camargo\, a 300-word car
 eer-stage rationale\, a CV (max 3 pages)\, work samples and two references
 . NOTE: the foundation has announced that this is the final Fellowship sel
 ected through a public open call until further notice\; a new selection pr
 ocess begins in July 2027\, making this the last chance to apply directly.
 \n\nOrganisation: Camargo Foundation\nLocation: Camargo Foundation campus\
 , Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.\nAward: EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 to
 tal for the ten-week residency)\, plus basic travel funding and a furnishe
 d apartment with equipped kitchen on the Camargo campus. 14 fellowships pe
 r year: 7 for artists and 7 for scholars and thinkers. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Ten weeks\, either 8 September to 17 November 2027 or 23 Febru
 ary to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fello
 wship-2026-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Camargo Foundation campus\, Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ars-biologica-artxscience-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ars Biologica ArtXScience Residency 2027-2028 (Ars Electr
 onica x Ceske Budejovice ECoC 2028)
DESCRIPTION:A fully funded art-science residency from Ars Electronica and C
 eske Budejovice\, European Capital of Culture 2028\, exploring how art and
  science can collaborate on urgent environmental challenges: climate emerg
 ence\, sustainable agriculture\, geoengineering\, permaculture\, water res
 ource management\, and the broader ecological impacts on local communities
 . Selected residents work in direct collaboration with local scientists in
  Ceske Budejovice. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, designers\, creatives and arti
 st collectives with more than 5 years of professional creative practice an
 d documented interdisciplinary work\; experience in community engagement a
 nd science collaborations is essential\; applicants must be 18+\, of any n
 ationality\, and legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. WHAT YOU G
 ET: a EUR 24\,000 package (EUR 6\,000 artist fee\, up to EUR 15\,000 produ
 ction\, up to EUR 3\,000 travel and subsistence) plus one month of housing
  covered separately\; two winners are selected. STRUCTURE: the residency r
 uns January 2027 to April 2028\, with a required on-site stay of up to one
  month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/September 2027\
 , and a March 2028 stay for festival installation\, exhibition and deinsta
 llation. HOW TO APPLY: online form with project title\, a written proposal
  of max 250 words\, a scientific direction statement (max 100 words)\, art
 istic vision (max 100 words)\, community connection plan (max 100 words)\,
  a super short pitch (3 to 30 words)\, 3 to 7 images\, an optional 1-minut
 e video\, a portfolio link and contact information. Two-round selection (o
 nline application then interview)\; winners notified by November 2026. Dea
 dline 28 September 2026 at midnight CET.\n\nOrganisation: Ars Electronica 
 and Ceske Budejovice - European Capital of Culture 2028\nLocation: Ceske B
 udejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any nationality aged 18+
  who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Required on-site s
 tays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/
 September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibition and deinstal
 lation.\nAward: EUR 24\,000 total package per winner: EUR 6\,000 artist fe
 e\, up to EUR 15\,000 production budget\, and up to EUR 3\,000 for travel\
 , accommodation and subsistence\, plus a separate budget covering one mont
 h of housing in Ceske Budejovice. Two positions available. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: January 2027 to April 2028\, with on-site blocks in early 2027 
 and March 2028\n\nMore: https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars
 -biologica-art-science-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars-biologica-art-scienc
 e-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ceske Budejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any natio
 nality aged 18+ who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Req
 uired on-site stays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional 
 stay in August/September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibiti
 on and deinstallation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surface-design-association-air-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: Surface Design Association: Artist in Residence 2026-27 (
 virtual)
DESCRIPTION:The Surface Design Association's Artist in Residence programme 
 is built to develop skills\, open up relationships and support fiber and t
 extile artists through a studio project that pushes their practice. The re
 sidency is entirely virtual and runs across a full year\, which is the poi
 nt: it removes the travel\, housing and time-away barriers that keep many 
 practitioners out of in-person residencies. One artist is selected annuall
 y by a panel of SDA staff\, board members and committee volunteers. WHAT I
 T INVOLVES: studio visits across the year\, social media programming\, and
  meetings with the SDA community\, plus further programming shaped around 
 the resident's own skills and interests\, which can include teaching onlin
 e workshops\, contributing to SDA publications\, jurying exhibitions or jo
 ining panels. FUNDING: a $2\,000 studio stipend for the project\, plus up 
 to $2\,000 in additional compensation for programming work\, targeting abo
 ut $4\,000 total. ELIGIBILITY: emerging\, mid-career and established artis
 ts\; US-based and international applicants welcome\; students and professi
 onals may apply\; applicants must hold current SDA membership (from $85 pe
 r year\, $60 for students). APPLICATION: a portfolio of 10 images with ful
 l artwork information (title\, year\, materials\, technique\, dimensions\,
  photo credit)\, a detailed CV\, essay responses on artistic goals\, progr
 amming interests and professional development\, and demographic informatio
 n. No application fee. The 2026-27 cycle closes 14 August 2026.\n\nOrganis
 ation: Surface Design Association (SDA)\nLocation: Fully virtual. Open to 
 US-based and international applicants\; current SDA membership is required
 .\nAward: $2\,000 studio stipend\, plus up to $2\,000 further compensation
  for programming work (SDA aims for roughly $4\,000 in total per resident)
 . No application fee\, but applicants must be current SDA members (members
 hip from $85 per year\, $60 for students).\nDuration: One year\, virtual\,
  October 2026 to August 2027\n\nMore: https://www.surfacedesign.org/air_fo
 rm/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.surfacedesign.org/air_form/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully virtual. Open to US-based and international applicants\; cur
 rent SDA membership is required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:instituto-sacatar-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Instituto Sacatar: Sacatar Fellowship 2027 (Bahia\, Brazi
 l)
DESCRIPTION:Brazil's longest-running artist residency\, on the island of It
 aparica across the bay from Salvador\, hosting six to eight artists per se
 ssion for roughly eight weeks. Open to all creative disciplines and\, unus
 ually for this region\, to all nationalities\, which makes it one of the f
 ew genuinely international residencies in Latin America. Sacatar provides 
 full hosting (private room\, three meals daily\, individual studio\, laund
 ry\, local logistics and connections) but explicitly does NOT provide a st
 ipend\, airfare to Salvador\, art materials or discretionary local expense
 s\, so applicants must fund their own travel. Individual artists only\; gr
 oup proposals are not accepted and former Sacatar Fellows may not reapply.
  COVID vaccination is required. NOTE: there is a USD 25 application fee. A
 pply through SlideRoom by 28 August 2026. Contact info@sacatar.org.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Instituto Sacatar\nLocation: Itaparica island\, Bahia\, Brazil.
  Open to all nationalities.\nAward: Fully hosted residency: private bedroo
 m with attached bathroom\, three meals a day (except Saturday evenings\, S
 undays and Brazilian holidays)\, an individual studio suited to the discip
 line\, weekly laundry\, logistical support and airport transfer from Salva
 dor. NO STIPEND\, no airfare and no materials budget. USD 25 application f
 ee. One French artist may additionally receive airfare and a stipend throu
 gh Villa Fatumbi.\nDuration: Approximately eight weeks\; specific session 
 dates are listed in the application.\n\nMore: https://sacatar.org/en/fello
 wships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sacatar.org/en/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Itaparica island\, Bahia\, Brazil. Open to all nationalities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:padimai-onchain-artist-residency-oops-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Padimai: OOPS Onchain Artist Residency 2026
DESCRIPTION:OOPS (Origin Of Primary Source) is the first edition of Padimai
  Art and Tech Studio's Onchain Artist Residency\, a three-month remote res
 idency inviting art practitioners to critically and creatively engage with
  NFTs as artistic registers and as systems of knowledge\, documentation\, 
 and access. Five practitioners are selected. SUPPORT: SGD 15\,000 honorari
 um per artist\, plus SGD 5\,000 per artist for documentation. The residenc
 y is remote\, with community engagement designed through online meetings\;
  at the end\, participants deliver three NFTs developed during the residen
 cy to Padimai. ELIGIBILITY: individual creative practitioners working at t
 he intersection of art\, technology\, and blockchain\; open internationall
 y. RESIDENCY: 20 September to 20 December 2026. DEADLINE: 15 August 2026. 
 Apply via the residency page.\n\nOrganisation: Padimai Art and Tech Studio
 \nLocation: Remote residency (Padimai is based in Singapore)\; open intern
 ationally.\nAward: SGD 15\,000 honorarium per artist plus an additional SG
 D 5\,000 per artist for documentation (audio\, video\, photography). Five 
 practitioners selected. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 months (20 Septem
 ber to 20 December 2026)\n\nMore: https://padimai.net/onchain-artist-resid
 ency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://padimai.net/onchain-artist-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote residency (Padimai is based in Singapore)\; open internatio
 nally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:oif-artist-mobility-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: OIF: Support for Artist Mobility and Circulation of Cultu
 ral Goods 2026 (Francophonie)
DESCRIPTION:The OIF Support for Artist Mobility and Circulation of Cultural
  Goods programme provides grants of up to EUR 5\,000 for mobility (travel\
 , accommodation\, visa fees\, logistics) and up to EUR 2\,500 for circulat
 ion of cultural goods (excess baggage\, freight\, customs) within the Fran
 cophone zone\; both can be combined. ELIGIBILITY: professional artists and
  established artistic collectives with a demonstrated connection to the Fr
 ancophone space (by nationality\, working language\, or artistic collabora
 tions)\, residing in an OIF member state\, with minimum 2 years of experie
 nce in the cultural or artistic sector\; must directly manage the project 
 (not act as intermediaries)\; one application per individual per call. ELI
 GIBLE DISCIPLINES: live performance (music\, theatre\, dance\, comedy\, st
 orytelling)\, cinema/audiovisual\, literature/publishing\, and visual arts
 . PRIORITY given to projects promoting gender equality\, women artists\, c
 reators under 35\, and South-South mobility. Projects must take place betw
 een 25 March and 30 November 2026. Multiple selection sessions throughout 
 the year\; call open until 31 August 2026. Information webinars: 11 March\
 , 13 May\, and 8 July 2026 at 3 PM Paris time. Contact: culture.dlc@franco
 phonie.org. Apply at https://appelsaprojets.francophonie.org/fr/applicatio
 ns/soutien-a-la-circulation-des-biens-et-mobilite-des-artistes.\n\nOrganis
 ation: Organisation internationale de la Francophonie (OIF)\nLocation: Fra
 ncophone zone (OIF member states globally)\nAward: Up to EUR 5\,000 for ar
 tist mobility (travel\, accommodation\, visas\, logistics). Up to EUR 2\,5
 00 for circulation of cultural goods (excess baggage\, freight\, customs).
  Both can be combined. No application fee.\nDuration: Residencies or tours
  of 5 days to 3 months. Projects must take place between 25 March and 30 N
 ovember 2026.\n\nMore: https://appelsaprojets.francophonie.org/fr/applicat
 ions/soutien-a-la-circulation-des-biens-et-mobilite-des-artistes?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://appelsaprojets.francophonie.org/fr/applications/soutien-a-la-ci
 rculation-des-biens-et-mobilite-des-artistes?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Francophone zone (OIF member states globally)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:she-got-game-emerging-talents-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Game Design Emerging Talents: She Got Game
DESCRIPTION:She Got Game is a Pro Helvetia mentoring programme promoting eq
 ual opportunities in the Swiss and international game industry\, supportin
 g the representation of women in management positions through dialogue wit
 h experienced international experts from the game industry. The call is ai
 med at professional game designers who identify as women from the regions 
 of Pro Helvetia's liaison offices: the Arab region\, East Asia\, South Ame
 rica\, South Asia and Vietnam\, and West\, Central\, East and Southern Afr
 ica. ELIGIBILITY: Emerging Talents are game developers or game studios who
 se relevant training or registration (self-employed\, sole proprietorship\
 , or company) dates back less than seven years. Selection criteria include
  digital content for devices such as computers\, consoles\, mobile devices
 \, or mixed-reality devices requiring a high level of interaction\; clearl
 y defined objectives in terms of participation and career\; originality\, 
 feasibility\, degree of innovation\, and marketability of the project\; an
 d project leaders holding the copyright and rights of use. Excluded: commi
 ssioned work and projects that are part of an advertising or promotional c
 ampaign\, and purely technological projects without obvious narrative and/
 or aesthetic content. WHAT IS OFFERED: individual coaching and input talks
  on project development\, business strategy and finances\, marketing\, PR\
 , and pitch training\; involvement in a growing community of female game d
 evelopers\; potential access to international trade fairs\; CHF 3\,000 tow
 ard the production of a prototype or demo version\; and up to CHF 250 per 
 week toward childcare or children's travel expenses when attending interna
 tional trade fairs (a budget must be presented in advance). All modules ar
 e conducted in English\; the programme is a time-consuming commitment of r
 oughly 30 hours over six months\, plus potential additional time attending
  events or trade fairs. APPLICATION: submit a short CV (including educatio
 n/training\, nationality\, and residency status in Switzerland)\, a short 
 description of the current project (type and genre\, gameplay\, platform\,
  development status\, target audience\, with relevant visual material\, ma
 x. 10 A4 pages)\, a project portfolio\, and expectations regarding the pro
 gramme's objectives. All documents should be submitted in English\; if not
  possible\, applications may be submitted in German\, French\, or Italian.
  KEY DATES: applications open 1 July 2026\; submission deadline 1 Septembe
 r 2026\, 23:59 CEST\; notification of decision in November 2026\; earliest
  project start 1 January 2027. Apply via the Pro Helvetia application port
 al.\n\nOrganisation: Pro Helvetia (Swiss Arts Council)\nLocation: Remote/i
 nternational mentoring programme\; applicants must be based in one of Pro 
 Helvetia's liaison-office regions (Arab region\, East Asia\, South America
 \, South Asia and Vietnam\, and West\, Central\, East and Southern Africa)
 .\nAward: CHF 3\,000 toward the production of a prototype or demo version\
 , plus individual coaching and community access\; potential access to inte
 rnational trade fairs. When attending trade fairs\, applicants with childc
 are responsibilities can request up to CHF 250 per week for childcare or c
 hildren's travel expenses. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-month mentor
 ing programme\, approximately 30 hours total commitment\; notification in 
 November 2026\; earliest project start 1 January 2027.\n\nMore: https://pr
 ohelvetia.ch/en/find-support/game-design-emerging-talents-she-got-game-3/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://prohelvetia.ch/en/find-support/game-design-emerging-talents-she
 -got-game-3/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote/international mentoring programme\; applicants must be base
 d in one of Pro Helvetia's liaison-office regions (Arab region\, East Asia
 \, South America\, South Asia and Vietnam\, and West\, Central\, East and 
 Southern Africa).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:web-residencies-no-22-ignore-all-previous-instructions-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260808
SUMMARY:Deadline: Akademie Schloss Solitude: Web Residencies No. 22 - Ignor
 e All Previous Instructions
DESCRIPTION:Web Residencies No. 22\, 'Ignore All Previous Instructions\,' i
 s curated by !Mediengruppe Bitnik as part of Akademie Schloss Solitude's W
 eb Residencies program\, which since 2016 has supported young talents from
  the international digital scene as well as artists of all disciplines eng
 aging with web-based practices. For the tenth anniversary of the program\,
  this edition invites artists to create works that challenge extractive di
 gital platforms and AI systems\, exploring how to interrupt dominant techn
 ological logics\, from ad-tech surveillance to prompt injection vulnerabil
 ities\, and imagine alternative structures and communication methods. The 
 open call seeks proposals that hijack\, glitch\, and disrupt rather than c
 omply with existing systems\, drawing on strategies like Situationist deto
 urnement to map routes through and around the grids we are trapped within.
  Four to six proposals will be selected. BENEFITS: a four-week online resi
 dency\; a grant of EUR 1\,200\; a web space (microsite) on which the proje
 ct can be developed and published online\; support from a web/UX designer 
 and a programmer (in a supportive capacity only\, not to build an entire w
 ebsite)\; and participation in individual and group online events with the
  curators and Solitude fellows. Offline or hybrid formats (a reading sessi
 on\, panel\, exhibition\, or curated program) may follow. ELIGIBILITY: art
 ists of all disciplines may apply\, with no age limit\; submission is free
  of charge. The residency takes place exclusively online\, and selected fe
 llows agree to be available online during the four weeks. TIMELINE: the op
 en call runs from July 7 to August 7\, 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Akademie Sch
 loss Solitude\nLocation: Online (fully remote). Awarded by Akademie Schlos
 s Solitude\, Stuttgart\, Germany.\nAward: EUR 1\,200 grant for a four-week
  online residency\, plus a microsite to develop and publish the project\, 
 and support from a web/UX designer and a programmer in a supportive capaci
 ty.\nDuration: Four-week online residency.\n\nMore: https://application.ak
 ademie-solitude.de/fellowship-information/web-residencies?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://application.akademie-solitude.de/fellowship-information/web-res
 idencies?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online (fully remote). Awarded by Akademie Schloss Solitude\, Stut
 tgart\, Germany.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:roosevelt-good-life-resident-ai-finance-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: Good Life Residents Program: AI and Finance
DESCRIPTION:The Good Life Residents Program is a six-month Roosevelt Instit
 ute initiative that develops new leaders and elevates bold ideas across si
 x economic policy priorities under its Good Life Agenda. Each topic area h
 osts a cohort of 3 to 4 residents plus 1 to 2 senior Topic Advisors (acade
 mics\, policy leaders\, organizers or government practitioners) who provid
 e strategic and intellectual mentorship and moderate the closing event. Re
 sidents write a roughly 1\,500-word original policy essay advancing a peop
 le-centered policy vision against a topic-specific prompt\, take part in s
 tructured dialogue with peers\, Topic Advisors and Roosevelt Think Tank di
 rectors\, help prepare and appear at a culminating public event\, contribu
 te to press opportunities such as op-eds and reporter calls where applicab
 le\, and act as ambassadors for the Good Life Agenda in their professional
  and public networks. The current round\, the AI and Finance Residents Pro
 gram\, is run in partnership with Data & Society and asks for bold ideas o
 n how to shape\, redirect\, interrupt or make more visible and contestable
  the flows of capital into generative AI. Framing: breakneck investment in
  generative AI technology and infrastructure is distorting the stock marke
 t\, profit measures\, bond issuance\, the pace and composition of growth\,
  job creation and trade policy\, with seven tech companies accounting for 
 more than a third of S&P 500 market capitalisation and complex\, sometimes
  circular financial arrangements obscuring risk. Suggested lines of inquir
 y include what government interventions could improve public oversight\, r
 isk allocation and direction-setting in private markets\; how other invest
 ments could be coordinated and scaled if AI spending is crowding them out\
 ; what role public spending or new institutions could play in catalysing t
 echnology R&D designed to support workers\, sustainability and supply chai
 ns\; what democratic input mechanisms would keep state influence over capi
 tal flows less vulnerable to corruption\; and how federal AI policy should
  address public hostility to data centre investments alongside national se
 curity and competitiveness. Compensation is USD 3\,000 plus travel and acc
 ommodation for the culminating event. The AI and Finance round closed 12 A
 ugust 2026 (deadline per Opportunity Desk\; Roosevelt's own programme page
  did not state it). Earlier rounds (Social Security financing\, Federal Re
 serve powers\, housing) each ran their own short application window\, so a
  future round may open with a new deadline.\n\nOrganisation: Roosevelt Ins
 titute\, in partnership with Data & Society\nLocation: Remote. Travel may 
 be requested for select convenings and for the culminating public event. U
 S economic policy focus.\nAward: USD 3\,000 stipend\, plus travel and acco
 mmodation for the culminating event if needed. No application fee.\nDurati
 on: 6 months\, part-time at roughly 5 to 8 hours per month\n\nMore: https:
 //rooseveltinstitute.org/careers/good-life-resident-program/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://rooseveltinstitute.org/careers/good-life-resident-program/?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. Travel may be requested for select convenings and for the 
 culminating public event. US economic policy focus.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tokyo-biennale-2027-social-dive@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tokyo Biennale 2027: Social Dive open call
DESCRIPTION:Social Dive is the Tokyo Biennale 2027 open call for art projec
 ts and artist-in-residence proposals developed through research and engage
 ment with local communities across Tokyo. Participants immerse themselves 
 in the city's neighborhoods\, from temples and historic wooden buildings t
 o business districts\, train stations and shopping streets. Selected proje
 cts are developed at 11 designated sites: Toeizan Kaneiji Temple\, Yushima
  Seido\, Otemachi/Marunouchi/Yurakucho\, Kyobashi\, Nihonbashi\, Yaesu\, K
 anda\, Ueno Station\, Tokyo Dome City\, Akihabara Electric Town\, and Baku
 rocho/Higashi-Kanda. Proposals must respond to the biennale theme From Me 
 to Us and be presented during Tokyo Biennale 2027 (1 October to 11 Decembe
 r 2027). WHAT YOU GET: JPY 100\,000 honorarium\, round-trip travel up to J
 PY 300\,000\, accommodation in Tokyo\, project development support up to J
 PY 200\,000\, venue coordination\, interpretation and translation support\
 , and an invitation letter. WHO IS ELIGIBLE: artists\, curators\, research
 ers\, collectives and practitioners in related fields\, aged 18 or older\,
  from anywhere in the world\; must be available for on-site research\, col
 laboration and implementation in Tokyo with a residency of at least one mo
 nth including the opening\; one proposal per applicant. SELECTION: two-sta
 ge evaluation on theme responsiveness\, originality\, feasibility and comm
 unity collaboration. DEADLINE: 30 September 2026\, 23:59 JST. TO APPLY: vi
 a the online form with profile photo\, personal statement (150 words)\, tw
 o key visuals\, project summary and background\, detailed description (400
  words)\, budget breakdown and schedule\, and CV or portfolio (max 5 pages
 ). Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: Tokyo Biennale\nLocation: Tokyo\, Japan
 \, at 11 designated sites. Open worldwide with no restrictions on national
 ity\, residence or base of operations\; requires on-site research and a re
 sidency of at least one month including the opening period.\nAward: JPY 10
 0\,000 honorarium (incl. tax)\, round-trip travel expenses up to JPY 300\,
 000 (economy\, one person)\, accommodation in Tokyo\, project development 
 support up to JPY 200\,000 (incl. tax)\, plus venue coordination\, interpr
 etation and translation support and an invitation letter. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Flexible residency (minimum one month including the opening
  period)\, with projects presented during Tokyo Biennale 2027\, 1 October 
 to 11 December 2027\n\nMore: https://socialdive.jp/en/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://socialdive.jp/en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Tokyo\, Japan\, at 11 designated sites. Open worldwide with no res
 trictions on nationality\, residence or base of operations\; requires on-s
 ite research and a residency of at least one month including the opening p
 eriod.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mq-air-2027-vienna-whw@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: MQ Artist-in-Residence Programme 2027\, Vienna
DESCRIPTION:The MQ Artist-in-Residence (MQ AiR) programme offers artists th
 e opportunity to step away from everyday production pressure and focus on 
 a specific artistic interest or research project\, living and working in o
 ne of the MQ studios in the MuseumsQuartier in the heart of Vienna. This 2
 027 call is run with tranzit and the WHW Akademija (supported by Erste Fou
 ndation and the Igor Zabel Association). SUPPORT: EUR 1\,300 monthly stipe
 nd and a EUR 300 travel grant (EUR 500 for participants from outside the E
 U)\, plus the live-work MQ studio. TO APPLY: send your application with th
 e subject line 'MQ AiR 2027' to office@tranzit.org\; the full call details
  and application form are at akademija.whw.hr. DEADLINE: 15 September 2026
  at midnight.\n\nOrganisation: MuseumsQuartier Wien (MQ AiR)\, with tranzi
 t and WHW Akademija\nLocation: MuseumsQuartier\, Vienna\, Austria. Live-wo
 rk residency in one of the MQ studios\; non-EU participants are eligible (
 higher travel grant).\nAward: EUR 1\,300 monthly stipend plus a EUR 300 tr
 avel grant (EUR 500 for non-EU participants). Live-work studio at the Muse
 umsQuartier included.\nDuration: Residency during 2027 in an MQ studio in 
 Vienna\n\nMore: https://www.akademija.whw.hr?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.akademija.whw.hr?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
LOCATION:MuseumsQuartier\, Vienna\, Austria. Live-work residency in one of 
 the MQ studios\; non-EU participants are eligible (higher travel grant).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stigler-center-journalists-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260920
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260921
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stigler Center: Journalists in Residence Program Spring 2
 027 (Chicago Booth)
DESCRIPTION:The Stigler Center at the University of Chicago Booth School of
  Business invites journalists from around the world to its Journalists in 
 Residence Program\, a roughly 12-week residency on the Hyde Park campus in
  spring 2027 focused on the interactions between politics\, business\, mar
 kets and the economy. Running since 2017\, the program has hosted journali
 sts from outlets including the BBC\, The New York Times and ABC News. WHAT
  YOU DO: audit three Chicago Booth classes\, participate actively in class
  discussions and complete required readings and assignments\, attend Stigl
 er Center seminars\, lectures\, conferences and other activities\, collabo
 rate with peers\, network with faculty and researchers\, and explore oppor
 tunities to write for the Center's ProMarket publication. WHAT YOU GET: US
 D 14\,000 stipend for living expenses\, economy-class airfare reimbursemen
 t\, SEVIS and visa application fee coverage\, health insurance through UCh
 icago\, housing guidance\, academic opportunities and a certificate upon c
 ompletion. Note that the stipend is subject to up to 14 percent tax withho
 lding for international participants and arrives after roughly the first m
 onth\, so you need funds to bridge that period. ELIGIBILITY: open to journ
 alists from all countries and media backgrounds\, including freelancers an
 d editors\, with some years of professional experience\, proficiency in En
 glish and a genuine interest in political economy and business reporting. 
 Successful applicants are notified by the end of December 2026. DEADLINE: 
 20 September 2026\, 11:59 pm Chicago time (CT).\n\nOrganisation: Stigler C
 enter\, University of Chicago Booth School of Business\nLocation: Universi
 ty of Chicago Booth School of Business\, Hyde Park campus\, Chicago\, USA.
  Open to journalists from all countries and media backgrounds.\nAward: USD
  14\,000 stipend to cover living expenses over the 12-week program\, plus 
 reimbursement of economy-class airfare to and from Chicago\, SEVIS and vis
 a application fees\, health insurance through UChicago\, and housing guida
 nce. The stipend is subject to up to 14 percent tax withholding for intern
 ational participants\, and you need sufficient funds for roughly the first
  month before the stipend arrives. No application fee.\nDuration: Approxim
 ately 12 weeks\, tentatively 15 March to 31 May 2027\n\nMore: https://www.
 chicagobooth.edu/research/stigler/education/journalists-in-residence-progr
 am?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.chicagobooth.edu/research/stigler/education/journalists-in-
 residence-program?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:University of Chicago Booth School of Business\, Hyde Park campus\
 , Chicago\, USA. Open to journalists from all countries and media backgrou
 nds.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eyebeam-agency-virtual-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261006T202434Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261011
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261012
SUMMARY:Deadline: Eyebeam AGENCY Virtual Residency\, Fall 2026 (US-based ar
 tists)
DESCRIPTION:Eyebeam's 2026-27 program year runs under the theme AGENCY: pre
 serving human agency as AI systems increasingly act\, decide and carry out
  tasks on our behalf\, asserting agency in imagining and building the soci
 eties we inhabit\, and rethinking agency as collective\, relational\, enta
 ngled and more-than-human. The Virtual Residency (Fall/Winter) supports US
 -based artists working outside New York City with time\, community and str
 ucture to develop new work around the theme\; it is well-suited to early-s
 tage projects where shared research\, discussion groups and feedback on na
 scent ideas would advance concept development and prototyping. Projects mi
 ght engage AI directly but do not have to\; Eyebeam welcomes critical and 
 creative approaches to power\, autonomy\, technological systems\, collecti
 ve action\, human and nonhuman relationships\, and the structures through 
 which agency is exercised\, including reimagining AI systems through colle
 ctive governance\, repair\, rehearsal or mischief\, and repurposing AI tow
 ard tools for the public good. STRUCTURE: six artists\, 12 weeks\, minimum
  3-4 hours per week\, with weekly cohort sessions\, independent project de
 velopment\, work-in-progress shares with peers and guest critics\, a closi
 ng virtual process presentation\, and a contribution to an end-of-year pub
 lication. ELIGIBILITY: individuals only (no collectives this cycle)\; not 
 enrolled in a full-time degree program\; not committed to another residenc
 y during the term\; US-based outside NYC for the full term. TIMELINE: dead
 line Sunday 11 October 2026\, 11:59 PM ET (no late applications)\; intervi
 ews 26-28 October\; notifications 29 October\; residency 4 November 2026 t
 o 12 February 2027. Info session Monday 28 September 2026\, 1 PM ET. Apply
  via Submittable\, free.\n\nOrganisation: Eyebeam\nLocation: Online. Appli
 cants must be based in the United States\, OUTSIDE New York City\, and rem
 ain US-based for the full residency term (4 November 2026 to 12 February 2
 027).\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend for each of six selected artists\, plus w
 eekly residency programming\, mentorship with Eyebeam alumni and visiting 
 professionals\, technical consultation\, and access to tools and expertise
  through Eyebeam's network. No application fee (via Submittable).\nDuratio
 n: 12 weeks\, 4 November 2026 to 12 February 2027\; minimum commitment 3-4
  hours per week.\n\nMore: https://eyebeam.org/articles/agency-virtual-resi
 dency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://eyebeam.org/articles/agency-virtual-residency?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online. Applicants must be based in the United States\, OUTSIDE Ne
 w York City\, and remain US-based for the full residency term (4 November 
 2026 to 12 February 2027).
END:VEVENT
